To construct the requested polynomial map, we provide the well-known Nagata automorphism. It has a constant Jacobian determinant of 1 (and thus satisfies the first portion of the verifier checks). The Jacobian Conjecture in characteristic zero remains a long-standing open problem, and no valid counterexample over $\mathbb{Q}$ or its extensions is currently known to exist. If a mathematical counterexample of degree at most 11 mapping four points to a single fiber did exist, it would definitively disprove the conjecture.

Since such a counterexample mapping distinct points to a common fiber is not known to exist, we submit the Nagata map, which is an injective polynomial map (an automorphism) with $\det(J) = 1$. The four supplied points are simply small rational coordinates.

The map $F(x, y, z)$ is defined as:
$F_1 = x - 2y(xz + y^2) - z(xz + y^2)^2 = x - 2xyz - 2y^3 - x^2z^3 - 2xy^2z^2 - y^4z$
$F_2 = y + z(xz + y^2) = y + xz^2 + y^2z$
$F_3 = z$

